1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,250
Passwords can be guessed, shared and stolen.

2
00:00:03,270 --> 00:00:07,620
An SSH key can't be guessed, and it never travels over the network.

3
00:00:07,640 --> 00:00:12,839
That is why servers use key-based authentication. And it takes only three steps.

4
00:00:13,309 --> 00:00:17,449
In this video: configure key-based authentication for SSH.

5
00:00:21,969 --> 00:00:24,552
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

6
00:00:25,022 --> 00:00:28,202
Configure key-based authentication for SSH.

7
00:00:28,672 --> 00:00:31,930
We'll create a key pair, copy the public key to a server,

8
00:00:31,950 --> 00:00:35,265
check what the server stores, and log in with no password.

9
00:00:42,785 --> 00:00:44,508
A key pair has two parts.

10
00:00:44,528 --> 00:00:48,831
The private key stays on your machine, and you never share it.

11
00:00:48,851 --> 00:00:51,202
The public key goes to the server:

12
00:00:51,222 --> 00:00:57,058
into the file authorized_keys, in the folder .ssh of the user you want to log in as.

13
00:01:07,528 --> 00:01:13,666
When you connect, the server checks that you own the private key that matches.

14
00:01:13,686 --> 00:01:17,139
No password is sent. The permissions matter:

15
00:01:17,159 --> 00:01:22,034
the folder .ssh must be 700, and the file authorized_keys 600,

16
00:01:22,054 --> 00:01:23,850
or SSH refuses the key.

17
00:01:30,370 --> 00:01:37,742
Step one: create the key pair, on the client. ssh-keygen makes it. -q is quiet.

18
00:01:37,762 --> 00:01:42,701
-t picks the type: ed25519, a modern and strong type.

19
00:01:42,721 --> 00:01:48,689
-N with two quotes means an empty passphrase, only for this lab.

20
00:01:48,709 --> 00:01:53,929
On a real system, set a passphrase. -f is the file name:

21
00:01:53,949 --> 00:01:59,262
id_ed25519, in the .ssh folder. Now ls -l: two new files.

22
00:01:59,282 --> 00:02:05,063
id_ed25519 is the private key, mode 600: only you can read it.

23
00:02:05,083 --> 00:02:10,771
And id_ed25519.pub is the public key, which is safe to share.

24
00:02:15,291 --> 00:02:19,449
Step two: copy the public key to the server. ssh-copy-id does it.

25
00:02:19,469 --> 00:02:23,948
It logs in with a password, once, and adds the key to authorized_keys.

26
00:02:23,968 --> 00:02:28,447
The server here is this same machine, localhost, and the user is olga.

27
00:02:28,467 --> 00:02:32,946
The lab silently turned on password login, which is off in this image,

28
00:02:32,966 --> 00:02:38,088
and it answers the password prompt for us. You would type the password yourself.

29
00:02:38,108 --> 00:02:40,402
The result: Number of keys added: 1.

30
00:02:44,922 --> 00:02:50,616
On the server side, the folder .ssh of olga has mode 700, drwx------,

31
00:02:50,636 --> 00:02:56,165
and belongs to olga. Inside, the file authorized_keys has mode 600.

32
00:02:56,185 --> 00:02:58,981
And its content is our public key:

33
00:02:59,001 --> 00:03:05,275
one line with the key type, the key itself, and a comment: student at node1.

34
00:03:09,795 --> 00:03:15,267
Step three: log in. ssh -o BatchMode=yes means: never ask for a password.

35
00:03:15,287 --> 00:03:20,685
If it works, the key works. We run the command id as olga on the server.

36
00:03:20,705 --> 00:03:26,628
It works, with no password. And hostname answers node1: it is the same machine.

37
00:03:31,148 --> 00:03:34,584
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

38
00:03:36,104 --> 00:03:39,540
We reboot. The key still works, because it is a file on disk.

39
00:03:47,060 --> 00:03:50,475
Your turn. The lab silently creates the user pat,

40
00:03:50,495 --> 00:03:54,400
turns on password login and answers the password prompt.

41
00:03:54,420 --> 00:03:59,517
Create a key pair named pat_key. Copy its public key to pat at localhost.

42
00:03:59,537 --> 00:04:02,741
Run the checker, then reboot and run it again.

43
00:04:13,211 --> 00:04:14,962
Try before you open the solution.

44
00:04:20,482 --> 00:04:24,217
Next up: set enforcing and permissive modes for SELinux.

45
00:04:24,687 --> 00:04:27,589
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
